{"query": "What documents are acceptable for NRI KYC video verification?", "context": "Section 4.2(a) of RBI Master Direction on KYC - For Non-Resident Indians (NRIs), overseas passport, valid Indian visa, and notarized overseas utility bill/work permit serve as Officially Valid Documents (OVDs). Video-based Customer Identification Process (V-CIP) can be performed provided the geolocation check confirms overseas residency and live liveness detection is verified.", "response": "Under Section 4.2(a) of the RBI Master Direction on Know Your Customer (KYC), Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) may use the following as Officially Valid Documents (OVDs) for Video-based Customer Identification Process (V-CIP): (1) Overseas passport, (2) Valid Indian visa, and (3) Notarized overseas utility bill or work permit. The V-CIP must confirm geolocation showing overseas residency and pass live liveness detection checks. Compliance Officers should ensure the V-CIP platform is RBI-approved and that all session recordings are archived per the bank's data retention policy. This matter should be reviewed by the Bank Internal Audit team and the Chief Compliance Officer (CCO)."}

{"query": "Can a bank store transaction data in a public cloud outside India?", "context": "Section 8.1 of RBI Master Direction on IT Governance - All regulated entities (REs) storing banking transaction and account master data in commercial public cloud environments must ensure primary active and disaster recovery (DR) data residues remain within Indian geographical borders. Cloud service providers must be MeitY-empanelled.", "response": "Under Section 8.1 of the RBI Master Direction on Information Technology Governance, regulated entities (REs) are prohibited from storing primary active banking transaction data or account master data in cloud infrastructure outside Indian geographical borders. Both the primary and disaster recovery (DR) copies must reside within India. Furthermore, the cloud service provider must be MeitY-empanelled. Banks using Azure, AWS, or GCP must verify their selected regions are MeitY-compliant (e.g., Azure Central India region). Non-compliance may attract regulatory action under the IT Act and RBI supervisory review. This should be escalated to the CCO and IT Risk team for immediate verification."}

{"query": "What are the restrictions on outsourcing CISO functions to a third party?", "context": "Section 6.3 of RBI Master Direction on Outsourcing of IT Services - Regulated entities shall not outsource core management functions including Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) oversight, compliance auditing, and final credit approval. Third-party FinTech vendors must submit to regular SAS-70 or SOC-2 Type II audit inspections.", "response": "Under Section 6.3 of the RBI Master Direction on Outsourcing of Information Technology Services, regulated entities are explicitly prohibited from outsourcing the following core management functions: (1) Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) oversight responsibilities, (2) Compliance auditing, and (3) Final credit approval decisions. These must remain within the bank's direct organizational control. Any third-party FinTech vendors engaged for other IT services must undergo regular SAS-70 or SOC-2 Type II audit inspections. Banks should maintain an active vendor risk management program. This policy must be reviewed annually by the CCO and reported in the Board Risk Committee minutes."}

{"query": "Can a merchant store the 16-digit card PAN after payment authorization?", "context": "Section 5.4 of RBI Master Direction on Digital Payment Security Controls - No entity in the payment chain other than card issuers and card networks shall store actual card credentials (16-digit PAN, CVV, Expiry) after transaction authorization. All merchant checkouts must use RBI-approved Token Service Providers (TSPs).", "response": "Under Section 5.4 of the RBI Master Direction on Digital Payment Security Controls, merchants and payment aggregators are strictly prohibited from storing actual card credentials — including the 16-digit PAN, CVV, and card expiry date — after transaction authorization is complete. Only card issuers and card networks are permitted to retain this data. All merchant checkout integrations must migrate to card-on-file tokenization (CoFT) using an RBI-approved Token Service Provider (TSP). Non-compliance constitutes a serious breach of payment security norms and may result in suspension of payment processing licenses. The CCO and CTO should jointly audit all payment integrations to confirm full tokenization compliance."}
